#version 460

// ===========================================================================
// WAVEFRONT SCATTER -- THE FORWARD INSTANTIATION
// ===========================================================================
//
// This file used to BE the scatter stage. It is now one of two instantiations
// of it: everything the stage does -- extensions, includes, bindings, the
// Push block, the local size, the constants, the RNG reconstruction, the
// BSDF, the environment sample, next-event estimation and MIS, the path
// advance and the re-queue -- lives in shaders/includes/diff/traverse.glsl,
// and the OTHER instantiation (shaders/diff/wf_scatter_replay.comp) includes
// the identical text.
//
// WHY. Spec section 6.2: the backward/replay kernel must walk the identical
// path, consuming the identical RNG values in the identical order, and
// divergence by a single RNG call means every gradient is silently wrong --
// no crash, no NaN, no diagnostic. Making that impossible STRUCTURALLY, by
// having exactly one copy of the source, is cheaper than making it unlikely
// by discipline. Read traverse.glsl's banner for why the bindings and the
// Push block moved there too and are not merely "shared by convention".
//
// The ONLY thing this file contributes is the body of `diffVertexHook` --
// the per-vertex hook traverse.glsl declares and calls exactly once per
// invocation. For the FORWARD pass that hook is the film accumulation, which
// is what this stage always did at that point in the traversal, character
// for character. For the replay pass it is (in this task) nothing at all.
//
// `diff_gpu_probe.cpp`'s `checkTraverseInstantiationTie()` reads both `.comp`
// files at startup and fails the whole probe if either one grows a
// `layout(...)` declaration, a second `#include`, or a function other than
// `diffVertexHook` and `main` -- so "the only difference between the forward
// and the replay stage is the hook" is measured, not claimed here.

#include "diff/traverse.glsl"

// --- THE FORWARD HOOK: FILM ACCUMULATION (Stage 0b-2b Task 5) -------------
//
// The MIS-combined direct-lighting estimate at this vertex, carried by the
// arrival throughput. Zero on the miss path by construction (traverse.glsl
// leaves both `Li` and `throughput` at zero there), so this is evaluated and
// added UNCONDITIONALLY: "every invocation writes the film on every bounce"
// is a simpler invariant to reason about than a branch, and it is the
// invariant the barrier note below is stated against.
//
// `v.throughput * v.Li` is the same product, in the same order, that this
// shader computed inline as
//
//     arrivalThroughput * (neeTerm.wOwn * neeTerm.unweighted +
//                          bsdfTerm.wOwn * bsdfTerm.unweighted)
//
// before the traversal was extracted; traverse.glsl forms the parenthesised
// half into `v.Li` and hands it over. The extraction is therefore bit-exact,
// which is what lets checks 14/17's `== 0.0625` and checks 20-34 stand as the
// proof that it changed nothing.
//
// --- WHICH BARRIER ORDERS THIS BUFFER BETWEEN BOUNCES, AND WHY. NOTHING
// TESTS THIS -- read it, do not trust a green run.
//
// In a fused multi-bounce run (ohao::diff::WavefrontLoop::record) every
// bounce dispatches this shader again into the SAME command buffer, and every
// one of those dispatches read-modify-writes the same three floats at
// film[pixelIndex*3 .. +2]. That is a read-after-write AND a
// write-after-write across dispatch boundaries, and it is ordered by exactly
// one thing: barrier (7) in WavefrontLoop::recordCompactingStage --
// VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_COMPUTE_SHADER_BIT -> COMPUTE_SHADER,
// VK_ACCESS_SHADER_WRITE_BIT -> SHADER_READ | SHADER_WRITE -- recorded after
// every compacting dispatch and given this buffer through record()'s
// `extraBarrierBuffers` span. A VkBufferMemoryBarrier's memory scope covers
// only the buffers it NAMES, so a caller that allocates a film and forgets to
// pass it there gets the execution dependency and none of the
// availability/visibility: bounce k's accumulation is not guaranteed to be
// seen by bounce k+1's atomicAdd. Nothing fails. The driver serialises
// back-to-back compute on one queue, synchronization validation has been
// MEASURED silent on this subsystem (wavefront_loop.hpp's class comment:
// every compute-side barrier disabled, zero SYNC- diagnostics), and the image
// still looks right. Stage 0b-2a shipped precisely this bug on `debugDraws`.
//
// The destination access mask MUST include SHADER_WRITE, not just
// SHADER_READ. An atomicAdd is a read-modify-write; a read-only destination
// scope does not order it. That is rule 1 of the three ordering rules in
// wavefront_loop.hpp's class comment, and this write is its third instance in
// the loop after the two counter atomicAdds.
//
// The film also has to be ZEROED before the loop and made visible to this
// dispatch. record() does not do that -- it zeroes nothing it does not own --
// so the caller owes a vkCmdFillBuffer plus a TRANSFER_WRITE ->
// SHADER_READ|SHADER_WRITE barrier ahead of the loop, exactly as
// WavefrontBuffers::zero does for the buffers it owns. Skip it and the film
// accumulates onto whatever the allocator handed back.
//
// --- THE FILM IS NOT COVERED BY THE SEED INVARIANT. WHICH OPTION THIS
// SUBSYSTEM TOOK, AND WHY (spec section 4.5, resolved Stage 1 Task 1).
//
// The seed invariant -- "a path is a pure function of (pixel, sampleIndex,
// iterationSeed)" -- holds for the PATH and NOT for this buffer. At more than
// one sample per pixel, several paths belonging to one pixel atomicAdd the
// same three floats WITHIN a single dispatch, and float addition is not
// associative, so the accumulated value depends on a scheduling order the
// seed does not control. Nothing observes it today (every probe runs 1 spp),
// but a PRB forward/backward comparison at multi-spp would: it would present
// as a gradient that is ALMOST right and irreproducible between runs, which
// reads as a backward-pass bug while being an accumulation-order artefact.
//
// The spec offers three ways out: (a) replay at 1 spp per dispatch,
// (b) accumulate per path and reduce deterministically, or (c) accept it and
// document a tolerance covering atomic reordering.
//
// THIS SUBSYSTEM TAKES (a): ONE SAMPLE PER PIXEL PER DISPATCH, ALWAYS.
//
// Why (a) and not the others:
//
//   * It makes this buffer bit-reproducible rather than reproducible to a
//     tolerance. With one path per pixel per dispatch, the only adds landing
//     on a given pixel within one command buffer come from DIFFERENT bounces,
//     and those are separated by barrier (7) above -- so the order is the
//     dispatch order, which is fixed by the recording, not by scheduling.
//     Several samples are then several runs, summed outside this shader in
//     an order the host chooses. Option (c) would instead make Task 6's
//     convergence gate compare two Monte Carlo estimates through a tolerance
//     that has to absorb atomic reordering ON TOP of sampling noise, which
//     is exactly the kind of widened band that stops a check from being able
//     to fail.
//   * It costs nothing. It is already how every probe in this subsystem runs
//     (wf_generate.comp writes sampleIndex 0 for every path and one path per
//     pixel), so (a) is a constraint to KEEP and enforce, not a mechanism to
//     build. Option (b) -- a per-path radiance field plus a deterministic
//     reduction -- is a whole extra buffer, an extra pass and an extra
//     ordering contract, bought to make legal something this subsystem has no
//     reason to do.
//
// How it is ENFORCED rather than merely intended:
//
//   * `ohao::diff::WavefrontLoop::Config::filmPixelCount` and the guard below
//     mean a dispatch can only touch pixels it was told exist; the sample
//     multiplicity is a property of what wf_generate.comp seeded.
//   * `GpuProbeContext::runWavefrontReplayProbe` REFUSES to run unless
//     width*height == capacity -- i.e. exactly one path per pixel -- and says
//     so in its error text.
//   * The replay check MEASURES it: it histograms the pixel index every path
//     recorded in the binding-3 trace and fails unless every pixel appears
//     exactly once. A future change that seeds two samples per pixel into one
//     dispatch trips that, rather than quietly producing a film whose value
//     depends on the scheduler.
//
// This choice is BINDING on Task 6's convergence gate: it may compare films
// bit-exactly across runs of the same seed, and must sum multi-sample films
// on the host rather than accumulating them in one dispatch.
void diffVertexHook(inout DiffVertex v) {
    const vec3 filmContribution = v.throughput * v.Li;

    if (pc.filmPixelCount > 0u && v.pixelIndex < pc.filmPixelCount) {
        const uint fb = v.pixelIndex * 3u;
        atomicAdd(film.v[fb + 0u], filmContribution.x);
        atomicAdd(film.v[fb + 1u], filmContribution.y);
        atomicAdd(film.v[fb + 2u], filmContribution.z);
    }
}

void main() { diffTraverse(); }
